===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# [[thirsty|Thirsty]] for [[blood#Noun|blood]]: [[inexorably]] [[violent]] or [[eager]] for [[bloodshed]]; [[murderous]].
#: {{synonyms|en|bloodlusty|homicidal|murtherous|q3=archaic|murderous}}
#: {{antonyms|en|unbloodthirsty}}
#* {{RQ:Otway Venice Preserv'd|act=V|scene=i|page=53|passage=[I]t is, as I may so say, a sawcy Plot: and we all know, most Reverend Fathers, that what is sawce for a Goose is sawce for a Gander: Therefore, I say, as those '''bloud-thirsty''' Ganders of the conspiracy would have destroyed us Geese of the Senate, let us make haste to destroy them, so I humbly move for hanging— [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|volume=III|issue=246|date=12 December 1711|page=229|passage={{...}} [[w:Diodorus Siculus|Diodorus]] also relates of {{w|Caligula}}, predecessor to {{w|Nero}}, that his nurse used to moisten the nipples of her breast frequently with blood to make Caligula take the better hold of them; which, says Diodorus, was the cause that made him so '''blood-thirsty''' and cruel all his life-time after, that he not only committed frequent murder by his own hand, but likewise wished that all human kind wore but one neck that he might have the pleasure to cut it off.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|chapter=Stave I. Marley’s Ghost.|page=16|passage=The Lord Mayor, in the stronghold of the mighty Mansion House, gave orders to his fifty cooks and butlers to keep Christmas as a Lord Mayor's household should; and even the little tailor, whom he had fined five shillings on the previous Monday for being drunk and '''blood-thirsty''' in the streets, stirred up to-morrow's pudding in his garret, while his lean wife and the baby sallied out to buy the beef.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|editor=Oliver Optic [pseudonym; {{w|William Taylor Adams}}]|title=Bloodhounds|magazine=Our Boys and Girls: Oliver Optic’s Magazine|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Lee & Shepard|Lee and Shepard]], publishers;{{nb...|New York, N.Y.: Lee, Shepard and Dillingham}}|date=12 March 1870|volume=VII|issue=167|page=169|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vEYAAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA169|column=2|oclc=7388757|passage=The genuine "bloodhound" is not, naturally, the cruel, '''bloodthirsty''' animal he is generally supposed to be; nor is he the only dog that will hunt men. Like all pure hounds, he is mild, loving, and kind, and will hunt any game for which he is trained; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Alex McLevy|title=Making a Killing: The Brief Life and Bloody Death of the Post-''[[w:Scream (franchise)|Scream]]'' Slasher Revival|url=https://www.avclub.com/making-a-killing-the-brief-life-and-bloody-death-of-th-1819784777|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180305120228/https://www.avclub.com/making-a-killing-the-brief-life-and-bloody-death-of-th-1819784777|archivedate=5 March 2018|work={{w|The A.V. Club}}|date=27 October 2017|passage=The slasher narrative is as simple as a knife in the head: Some tragic event creates a killer who then seeks '''bloodthirsty''' revenge for that primal trauma, and audiences hoot and cringe in equal measure as characters lose their lives one by one, often in unexpected or inventive ways, until a resourceful “Final Girl” manages to defeat the monster.}}
# Of a [[book#Noun|book]], [[film#Noun|film]], etc.: depicting much [[violence]]; [[gory]], [[violent]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Oliver Lodge|title=The Religious Education of Children|editor=[[w:George Brinton McClellan Harvey|George Harvey]]|journal={{w|North American Review}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=The North American Review Publishing Co.|volume=CLXXXVI|issue=DCXX|date=2 August 1907|section=section III|page=707|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/northamreview185miscrich/page/707|oclc=925540419|passage=[T]here has been recently a tendency on the part of some Education Authorities to select these manifestly worthy portions exclusively, and to avoid reading the more archaic and, so to speak, '''bloodthirsty''' books, such as Judges, Kings, and Genesis, altogether.}}
# {{lb|en|jocular}} Of a [[mosquito]], tenaciously seeking to draw blood.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=H. Rider Haggard|authorlink=H. Rider Haggard|title=She: A History of Adventure|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3155/3155-0.txt|passage=For, whether they were attracted by the lantern, or by the unaccustomed smell of a white man for which they had been waiting for the last thousand years or so, I know not; but certainly we were presently attacked by tens of thousands of the most '''blood-thirsty''', pertinacious, and huge mosquitoes that I ever saw or read of.}}

